## 1️⃣ Go Implementation  

```go
// pipeline.go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

// ----- Types ---------------------------------------------------------

type Item struct {
	ID   int    // just a placeholder identifier
	Data string // the actual payload – could be anything
}

func NewItem(id, data string) *Item { return &Item{ID: id, Data: data} }

// ----- Channel types -----------------------------------------------

type ProducerChan chan *Item
type TransformerChan chan *Item
type ConsumerChan  chan *Item

// ----- Stage A – Producer -------------------------------------------------

// Simple producer that pushes a batch of items onto the channel.
func Producer(outc ProducerChan) {
	for i := 1; i <= 10; i++ { // ← change as needed
		outc <- NewItem(i, fmt.Sprintf("payload-%d", i))
	}
	close(outc)
}

// ----- Stage B – Transformer -------------------------------------------------

// Transform doubles the ID and appends a suffix.
func Transformer(inc TransformerChan) {
	for item := range inc {
		item.ID *= 2               // example transformation
		item.Data += " (transformed)"
		incoming <- item           // forward to consumer
	}
}

// ----- Stage C – Consumer -------------------------------------------------

// Consume prints each received item.
func Consumer(outc ConsumerChan) {
	for item := range outc {
		fmt.Printf("Item %d → %s\n", item.ID, item.Data)
	}
}

// ----- Main driver -----------------------------------------------------

func main() {
	const batchSize = 10

	incoming := make(TransformerChan, batchSize) // buffer size
	outgoing := make(ConsumerChan, batchSize)

	go Producer(outgoing)          // Stage A – producer writes to outgoing (consumer channel)
	go Transformer(incoming)       // Stage B – transformer reads from incoming (producer channel)
	Consumer(outgoing)            // Stage C – consumer reads from outgoing (transformer channel)

	fmt.Println("All done!")
}
```

### What the pipeline does  

| Step | Channel direction | Purpose |
|------|-------------------|---------|
| **A → B** | `ProducerChan` → `TransformerChan` (`incoming`) | Producer pushes raw items; transformer consumes them and emits transformed items. |
| **B → C** | `TransformerChan` → `ConsumerChan` (`outgoing`) | Transformer modifies data; consumer finally reads the final result. |

*All channels are buffered with a modest capacity (10) to illustrate buffering semantics.*  

